"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,529\nThe Learning Brain. Series 5: Belonging\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,314\nEpisode 9\n\n3\n00:00:07,394 --> 00:00:09,221\nSeeing yourself\n\n4\n00:00:12,021 --> 00:00:15,930\nCan seeing someone like you at the front of the room change where you end up?\n\n5\n00:00:16,417 --> 00:00:18,366\nIt is a hard question to test,\n\n6\n00:00:18,657 --> 00:00:22,259\nbecause students and teachers are rarely matched at random.\n\n7\n00:00:22,429 --> 00:00:25,138\nBut Tennessee's STAR experiment,\n\n8\n00:00:25,158 --> 00:00:30,592\nin the 1980s, randomly assigned young students and their teachers to classrooms,\n\n9\n00:00:30,825 --> 00:00:33,035\nfrom kindergarten through third grade.\n\n10\n00:00:33,585 --> 00:00:37,184\nYears later, that made a rare fair test possible.\n\n11\n00:00:37,354 --> 00:00:38,884\nEconomists followed up.\n\n12\n00:00:39,464 --> 00:00:45,951\nBlack students randomly assigned at least one Black teacher in those early grades were nine percentage points,\n\n13\n00:00:46,166 --> 00:00:50,140\nor thirteen percent, more likely to graduate from high school.\n\n14\n00:00:50,604 --> 00:00:51,981\nAnd they were six points,\n\n15\n00:00:52,001 --> 00:00:55,870\nor nineteen percent, more likely to enroll in college.\n\n16\n00:00:56,040 --> 00:00:58,332\nThere was no significant long run effect,\n\n17\n00:00:58,352 --> 00:01:00,513\neither way, for white students.\n\n18\n00:01:00,948 --> 00:01:04,869\nThe gains were concentrated among disadvantaged Black boys,\n\n19\n00:01:05,099 --> 00:01:08,627\nand the college gains came mostly from two year colleges.\n\n20\n00:01:09,031 --> 00:01:12,059\nData from North Carolina showed a similar pattern.\n\n21\n00:01:12,517 --> 00:01:17,116\nAnd these long run effects were not visible in short run test scores alone.\n\n22\n00:01:17,286 --> 00:01:20,477\nThe researchers suggest role model effects.\n\n23\n00:01:20,864 --> 00:01:27,109\nA teacher who shares your background can change your beliefs about what is possible for someone like you.\n\n24\n00:01:27,279 --> 00:01:28,251\nSome caution.\n\n25\n00:01:28,621 --> 00:01:30,496\nThis is about the earliest grades,\n\n26\n00:01:30,516 --> 00:01:32,125\nand one group of students.\n\n27\n00:01:32,389 --> 00:01:35,396\nAnd most instructors cannot change who they are.\n\n28\n00:01:35,721 --> 00:01:37,965\nBut you can change who your students see.\n\n29\n00:01:38,135 --> 00:01:38,851\nFor teaching.\n\n30\n00:01:39,283 --> 00:01:42,465\nInvite speakers and alumni from varied backgrounds.\n\n31\n00:01:42,772 --> 00:01:45,952\nFeature a range of people in your examples and readings.\n\n32\n00:01:46,225 --> 00:01:50,047\nAnd connect students with mentors and peers they can identify with.\n\n33\n00:01:50,542 --> 00:01:53,871\nLook beyond test scores when you judge whether it is working.\n\n34\n00:01:54,391 --> 00:01:59,527\nOne tip to share with students: find a mentor or peer you can see yourself in.\n\n35\n00:01:59,697 --> 00:02:01,692\nStudents need to see a future self.\n\n36\n00:02:01,882 --> 00:02:02,932\nShow them many.\n\n"
